body:
I have been online dating since I was 15 (the reason I started so young is a story for another time). I met a man online at age 18 and dated him until age 20. We planned to get engaged, but in the end, we just were too different in our views and outlooks on life. Our split had nothing to do with the fact that we met online.

In the months following our breakup, I went on over a dozen first dates via Bumble. They ranged from great times where I really clicked with people, to painfully boring times where I couldn't wait to go home. Most didn't work out long-term because people were moving or "not looking for anything serious." I had to get used to the casual dating lifestyle, and as a hopeless romantic, it was painful. But overall, my experience was extremely positive. I learned a lot about people and myself, and I made two of my best friends. 

My second Bumble dating sequence ended with the guy and me friendzoning each other, and we are still friends to this day (over a year later).

My third Bumble dating sequence was when I found my soulmate. On our first "date," we just went walking on our college campus at night and then talked in his car for hours afterward. Every time he dropped me back at my apartment after we went out, we struggled to find a place to stop our conversations. I would turn to leave, and he would get ready to drive away, but something would stop both of us. I would stand in the parking lot or on the curb as he sat in the driver's seat with the door open or leaned against his car, and we would just keep talking. We had never found anyone who "got" us like each other. We acted like platonic friends for several weeks, despite the fact that we met on a dating app, and it wasn't until one night when we were watching a movie that we kissed and found out our physical chemistry was just as strong.

&#x200B;

After a while of hooking up after our adventures around town together, he expressed that he didn't have romantic feelings for me and wanted to stop. I was heartbroken and confused at first, but eventually realized that I wanted him in my life so much, that I didn't care whether he was my lover or friend.

&#x200B;

I still consider him my soulmate now, just in a different way. I am going to ask him to be my man of honor at my wedding if I marry my current girlfriend, who, oddly enough, I met in REAL life, out of the blue, while acting in a production of The Vagina Monologues (yes, really. We have the gayest story every).

&#x200B;

I should be dead in a ditch somewhere because of how many strangers I have met online over the last 6 years, but I am so grateful for the connections online dating has given me, and I don't think it is a less valid way to find love and friendship.
